# Division by zero: the integer/float asymmetry, pinned.
#
# An INTEGER divisor of zero traps (T_DivByZero) because there is no answer in
# the integer domain. A FLOAT divisor of zero does NOT trap: it follows IEEE
# 754, yielding +/-inf for a nonzero numerator and NaN for 0.0/0.0.
#
# The asymmetry is representability, not convention -- float division by zero
# has a defined answer and merely raises a status flag, so producing inf is the
# correct result rather than a missed check. Every surveyed language splits it
# this way; Python is the outlier that raises for both, and NumPy declines to
# follow it because per-element exceptions make array math unusable.
#
# This fixture pins the FLOAT half, which runs to completion. The integer half
# traps and is pinned by the trap-code fixtures.

void main():
    float a = 1.0
    float b = 0.0
    float pos = a / b
    float neg = (0.0 - 1.0) / b
    float nan = b / b
    print(pos)
    print(neg)
    print(nan != nan)
